39	+A bootstrap from a `github.com` source without `--target-max-pack-bytes` first asks the GitHub REST API (`GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}`) for the repository size, sending the source credentials git-sync already holds so a private repository answers too (see [Credentials and redirects](#credentials-and-redirects)). At 1.5 GiB or more it switches to batches of 512 MiB (or `--max-pack-bytes`, if smaller). If the lookup fails, the bootstrap starts with a single pack and git-sync prints a notice naming the cause; it still switches to batches if the target rejects the pack as too large. The outcome goes to the library's `Options.BootstrapLogger` (warning level on failure) and, on success, to `-v`. Pass `--target-max-pack-bytes` to choose batching yourself and skip the lookup.
40	+
41	 ### Bootstrap chain ordering
42	 
43	 Batched bootstrap walks a chain of source commits and places sub-pack
44	@@ -393,6 +395,17 @@ If a redirect target legitimately needs credentials and is not same-site, store
45	 them in your git credential helper against that host; git-sync will find them
46	 there.
47	 
48	+The GitHub repository-size lookup described under [Examples](#examples) sends
49	+the credentials git-sync holds when it runs: a `--source-token`, a
50	+`user:token@` in the source URL, or credentials the helper supplied when the
51	+git server asked for them (a public repository never asks, so it is looked up
52	+anonymously). It follows the same rule, measured from the host each credential
53	+is bound to: credentials for `github.com` go to `api.github.com` and no other
54	+host, helper credentials looked up for a redirect host stay with that host, and
55	+with TLS verification off none are sent. A token the API refuses (401 or 403)
56	+is retried once without credentials. A `github.com` URL with a non-default port
57	+is not treated as GitHub and gets no lookup.
